.\" texrsall(1) — comprehensive all-in-one man page for texrs
.\" Modeled after zshall(1): every section of the reference in one document.
.\" Render with:  man -l man/man1/texrsall.1
.\" Install:      cp man/man1/texrsall.1 /usr/local/share/man/man1/
.TH TEXRSALL 1 "2026-08-30" "texrs 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
texrsall \- the texrs comprehensive reference (invocation, the mouth, the
expander, lowering, the bytecode VM, parity harnesses, gaps)
.SH OVERVIEW
This is the all\-in\-one
.B texrs
manual, modeled on
.BR zshall (1).
It concatenates the major sections of the reference into a single page so you
can grep with `/':
.RS 2
.PP
.B 1.  INVOCATION
.RB "\(em " "running a file, introspection flags"
.br
.B 2.  THE MOUTH
.RB "\(em " "category codes, the line scanner, ^^X"
.br
.B 3.  THE EXPANDER
.RB "\(em " "\edef, \ecsname, \ethe, the conditionals"
.br
.B 4.  REGISTERS
.RB "\(em " "\ecount, arithmetic, groups and the save stack"
.br
.B 5.  LOWERING
.RB "\(em " "what happens at compile time and what does not"
.br
.B 6.  BYTECODE VM
.RB "\(em " "fusevm slots, branches, the \emessage host calls"
.br
.B 7.  PARITY
.RB "\(em " "the differential corpus and the fuzzers"
.br
.B 8.  GAPS
.br
.B 9.  EXIT STATUS
.br
.B 10. FILES
.br
.B 11. EXAMPLES
.RE
.PP
For a shorter quick\-reference page see
.BR texrs (1).
.SH "1.  INVOCATION"
.B texrs
takes one file and any of the introspection flags:
.RS
.nf
texrs FILE.tex          run it, print the \emessage stream
texrs \-\-dump\-tokens F   print the mouth's token stream, then exit
texrs \-\-disasm F        print the lowered fusevm bytecode, then exit
texrs \-\-help            print the option grammar
texrs \-\-version         print the version banner
.fi
.RE
.PP
Output is written the way tex writes it on the terminal \(em the opened file
name, the messages, and the closing paren:
.RS
.nf
(./hello.tex HELLO\-WORLD )
.fi
.RE
.PP
That line is the parity contract for this milestone.  The engine banner tex
prints above it is deliberately not reproduced: texrs does not claim to be TeX
Live.
.SH "2.  THE MOUTH"
The mouth turns bytes into tokens under the category\-code table
.RB ( tex.web
\(sc303).  A character's category is not fixed by the language: it is a
mutable table entry, so
.B \ecatcode`\e{=1
is what makes
.B {
a group opener, and INITEX \(em which is what texrs starts from \(em leaves it
an ordinary character.  A file that wants groups says so first.
.PP
The scanner is a three\-state machine per line:
.B N
at the start of a line,
.B M
in the middle,
.B S
while skipping blanks.  The states are not decoration.  They are what makes a
blank line a
.BR \epar ,
what collapses a run of spaces to one, and what makes the space after a
control word vanish while the space after a control symbol stays.
.PP
.B ^^X
notation
.RB ( tex.web
\(sc352) is carried: a superscript character twice, then one character,
denotes a control character \(em which is how a line end gets written inside a
macro body.
.SH "3.  THE EXPANDER"
.TP
.B \edef \egdef \exdef \eedef
Macro definition, with undelimited
.RB ( #1 )
and delimited
.RB ( \(dq\edef\epair#1,#2.\(dq )
parameters, and
.B ##
for a literal parameter character.
.B \eedef
and
.B \exdef
expand the body at definition time;
.B \egdef
and
.B \exdef
are global.
.TP
.B \elet
.B \ea
takes
.BR \eb 's
CURRENT meaning, not a reference to it.
.TP
.B \ecsname\eendcsname
Builds a control sequence out of the characters between them, so a macro can
name another macro.
.TP
.B \estring, \ethe, \enumber
.B \estring
prints a control sequence as text;
.B \ethe
and
.B \enumber
print a register's value.
.TP
.B \eexpandafter, \enoexpand
One token of lookahead, and one token of suppression.
.TP
.B conditionals
.BR \eiftrue ,
.BR \eiffalse ,
.BR \eifnum ,
.BR \eifodd ,
.BR \eifx ,
.B \eifcase
with
.BR \eor ,
plus
.B \eelse
and
.BR \efi ,
nested, and usable inside a
.B \emessage
body.
.SH "4.  REGISTERS"
TeX has exactly 256
.B \ecount
registers
.RB ( tex.web
\(sc236) and texrs maps them onto VM slots 0..255, so a register read is an
array index.
.B \eadvance ,
.B \emultiply
and
.B \edivide
do the arithmetic;
.B \edivide
truncates toward zero, as TeX's does.
.PP
A brace group scopes the macro table AND the registers written inside it: the
lowered body is wrapped in save/restore for exactly the registers it assigns.
.B \eglobal
opts out.
.SH "5.  LOWERING"
.B \ecatcode
and
.B \edef
take effect at COMPILE time \(em they change how the rest of the file reads,
so they cannot be deferred to run time.  Everything whose value is only known
when the program runs becomes a command in the intermediate stream.
.PP
A conditional is the interesting case.  A tree\-walker decides the branch as it
goes; a compiler cannot, because the registers it tests are run\-time state.
So both arms are collected as token runs, lowered recursively, and emitted as a
real branch.  That is what makes
.B \eifnum
compile to a comparison plus a jump instead of a Rust
.BR if .
A conditional whose truth depends only on the macro table
.RB ( \eiftrue ,
.BR \eifx )
is folded while lowering, because there is nothing left for the VM to test.
.SH "6.  BYTECODE VM"
texrs is a
.B fusevm
frontend, not an interpreter:
.RS
.nf
mouth \(-> expander \(-> command stream \(-> fusevm bytecode \(-> VM + Cranelift JIT
.fi
.RE
.PP
A count register is a VM slot, so
.B \eadvance\ecount0 by 5
is
.B GetSlot / LoadInt / Add / SetSlot
\(em native ops the JIT can compile \(em rather than a hash lookup and a match
in a tree\-walker.
.B \emessage
is two host builtins: one appends a rendered piece, one flushes the assembled
string, which is why a message containing
.B \ethe\ecount0
reads the register at RUN time.
.PP
.B texrs \-\-disasm
prints the listing.
.SH "7.  PARITY"
The contract is the
.B \emessage
stream, compared byte\-for\-byte against the real
.B tex
binary.  No expectation is written by hand \(em the reference is produced by
running tex at test time \(em so a case cannot be made to pass by changing
texrs.
.TP
.B cargo run \-\-bin parity \-\- \-\-freeze
Record what the reference engine prints for every case into
.IR tests/data/parity_expected.txt ,
which
.I tests/parity.rs
replays with no TeX installed \(em which is how CI, where there is none,
verifies the corpus at all.
.TP
.B cargo test
Runs the differential corpus, the example programs, the bytecode assertions,
and the fuzz smoke and mass\-replay harnesses.
.TP
.B cargo run --bin parity
The same comparison as a script, over
.IR tests/cases .
.TP
.B cargo run \-\-bin parity\-fuzz \-\- \-\-programs 500
Generates seeded programs confined to the implemented subset and runs each
through both engines, packing 40 independent probes into every one because the
oracle costs half a second per invocation.  A divergence is minimized to the
single probe responsible before it is reported, and replays exactly with
.BR "\-\-seed N \-\-once" .
.TP
.B cargo +nightly fuzz run lower
Coverage\-guided fuzzing for panics rather than divergences; targets
.BR lex ,
.BR lower ,
and
.BR run .
.PP
Both shell harnesses read the tex version they were measured against out of
.B BUGS.md
and refuse to run against a different engine: a mismatched oracle does not fail
loudly, it reports a different divergence set that reads like a regression.
.SH "8.  GAPS"
No stomach: no boxes, glue, paragraphs, fonts or DVI.  No registers other than
.BR \ecount .
No format is preloaded, so every register starts at zero as INITEX leaves them,
while the reference
.B tex
has plain's values.  An undefined control sequence prints its name instead of
raising an error.
.B \eedef
does not decide a conditional in its body at definition time.
.B #{
parameter text is refused.  Each of these is pinned by a case in
.I tests/cases
and written down in
.I tests/known_gaps.txt
with its reason; the gate fails on any divergence that is NOT listed, and on a
listed case that has started passing.
.SH "9.  EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
Successful completion.
.TP
.B 1
The file could not be read, an option was not recognised, or the engine
stopped.  Errors are written to stderr as
.BR "! <reason>." .
.SH "10. FILES"
.TP
.I examples/*.tex
Runnable examples, each held in parity with real tex by the test suite.
.TP
.I tests/cases/*.tex
The differential corpus.
.TP
.I tests/known_gaps.txt
Every case that does not match tex yet, with the reason.
.TP
.I fuzz/corpus/*
Seed inputs for the cargo\-fuzz targets.
.SH "11. EXAMPLES"
.PP
A macro with a delimited parameter:
.RS
.nf
\ecatcode`\e{=1 \ecatcode`\e}=2 \ecatcode`\e#=6
\edef\epair#1,#2.{[#1|#2]}
\emessage{\epair 1,2.}
\eend
.fi
.RE
.PP
A conditional inside a message body, tested against a register:
.RS
.nf
\ecatcode`\e{=1 \ecatcode`\e}=2
\ecount2=5
\emessage{\eifnum\ecount2>3 BIG\eelse SMALL\efi}
\eend
.fi
.RE
